body:
Instead of

````java

        public void process(final InputStream in) {
            try (final InputStream is = in) {
                final Iterator<KafkaRecord> records = kafkaConverter.convert(attributes, is, inputLength);
                producerService.send(records, publishContext);
            } catch (final Exception e) {
                publishContext.setException(e); // on data pre-process failure, indicate this to controller service
                producerService.send(Collections.emptyIterator(), publishContext);
            }
        }
````

we can have:

````java
        public void process(final InputStream in) {
            try {
                final Iterator<KafkaRecord> records = kafkaConverter.convert(attributes, in, inputLength);
                producerService.send(records, publishContext);
            } catch (final Exception e) {
                publishContext.setException(e); // on data pre-process failure, indicate this to controller service
                producerService.send(Collections.emptyIterator(), publishContext);
            }
        }
````

Hi [user], this is a nice catch!  Thanks for noting the problem, and working through the process to contribute a fix.

I would encourage you to perform some manner of verification on the records sent by `PublishKafka`.  To help leverage the design of the existing integration tests, it would also be better to break out the the new test into a separate test class.

In order to speed up the suite of integration tests associated with the kafka processors, they all derive from `AbstractKafkaBaseIT`, which starts a single kafka server via `testcontainers`, which is used for all derived tests.  In order to prevent the tests from interfering with each other, the Kafka topic used for a test is derived from the test name.

```
runner.setProperty(PublishKafka.TOPIC_NAME, getClass().getName());
```

The current `PublishKafka` tests use NiFi's `TestRunner` to publish the payload under test, and then verify directly via kafka client library APIs.  There are several examples of this in the package you're already working in.

In the test publish method, this assertion would verify the expected record count associated with the test FlowFile:

```
        MockFlowFile flowFile = runner.getFlowFilesForRelationship(PublishKafka.REL_SUCCESS).getFirst();
        assertEquals(Integer.toString(EXPECTED_RECORD_COUNT), flowFile.getAttribute("msg.count"));
```

For the suggested consume method, you'll want to make a copy of an existing consume, with a couple of modifications:

- Rather than accept the default Kafka consume count of 500, you'll want to set that higher.

```
        final Properties kafkaConsumerProperties = getKafkaConsumerProperties();
        kafkaConsumerProperties.setProperty(MAX_POLL_RECORDS_CONFIG, Integer.toString(EXPECTED_RECORD_COUNT * 2));
```

- Even with this change, the Kafka consumer client chunks the records it consumes into smaller batches.  So rather than calling `consumer.poll()` once, you'll want to do multiple times.  Something like:

```
            final List<ConsumerRecord<String, String>> records = new ArrayList<>();
            for (int i = 0; (i < 5); ++i) {
                final ConsumerRecords<String, String> recordsPoll = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(1));
                recordsPoll.forEach(records::add);;
            }
            assertEquals(EXPECTED_RECORD_COUNT, records.size());
```

This works on my machine, but may need adjustment to work everywhere it needs to work.


